  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f: \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow$ B be a function defined as $f(x)=$ $\\frac{x-1}{x-2}$, where $A=R-\\{2\\}$ and $B=R-\\{1\\}$. Then $f$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "AIrCb",
      "text": "\ninvertible and $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{2 y+1}{y-1}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "VD3Ax",
      "text": "\ninvertible and $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{3 y-1}{y-1}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "IG5LI",
      "text": "\nno invertible\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "9fLQQ",
      "text": "\ninvertible and $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{2 y-1}{y-1}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Suppose $y=f(x)$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Rightarrow y=\\frac{x-1}{x-2} \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow y x-2 y=x-1 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow(y-1) x=2 y-1 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow x=f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{2 y-1}{y-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nAs the function is invertible on the given domain and its inverse can be obtained as above.",
    "image": null
  },
